! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1260 TEST 8B -- "CHKS DS WITH SYSTEMS
! DISABLED, AND ENABLED" -- labels 160123 through 160Z1, PDF p.75,
! octal 022513.00-022545.40.  One unbroken run of 52 rows (54 half-
! words: two of the rows are full-word VFL loads).  This deck's
! listing pages carry NO printed page numbers; cite by PDF page.
!
! THIS IS THE RICHER DS ORACLE.  programs/ax5b_16086_ds.strap already
! held IBM's DS test for "SV TO 1.0 FAILS TO SET DS WHEN ENABLED",
! nineteen half-words of it.  TEST 8B covers the same indicator in
! BOTH system states and adds the thing 16086 never touches: whether
! the offending store was SUPPRESSED.  Its three error messages are
! the contract, transcribed verbatim from p.75:
!
!   -SX OUT OF BOUNDS SETS DS WHEN     (022520.40-022521.00)
!   -SYSTEM DISABLED.
!
!   -NO INTERRUPT OCCURED ON AN        (022536.00-022536.40)
!   -SX ABOVE UB.
!
!   -SX ABOVE UB WAS NOT NOPED, DATA   (022540.40-022541.00)
!   -WAS STORED WHEN ENABLED.
!
! plus the housekeeping one at 022530.40-022531.00, "-UNABLE TO CLR
! IND REGS MUST / -TERMINATE THIS TEST."  ("OCCURED", one R, is the
! deck's own spelling.)
!
! THE THIRD MESSAGE IS THE ACCEPTANCE TEST FOR OPERATION TERMINATION.
! The block's second half runs ENABLED (BE at 022534.00) with the
! mask bit for DS left at zero, stores X2 -- loaded from 100O, the
! "INDX WD. OF ONES" constant at 024503 -- into 160AD1, which sits
! above the upper boundary the LVI/SV pair just planted, and then
! reads 160AD1 back with L(BU) and demands a ZERO result.  If the
! machine let the store land, the word is nonzero, BRZ does not
! branch, and the deck prints "SX ABOVE UB WAS NOT NOPED, DATA WAS
! STORED WHEN ENABLED."  That is IBM saying, in its own regression
! suite, that a protection violation must NOP the store, not merely
! set an indicator.
!
! HOW THE TWO HALVES DIFFER.  The first half (022515.40-022531.40) is
! the DISABLED case: BIT57 (the boundary-control bit) into X1, SX to
! word 3.0 arms boundary control, then "SX,$X1,160AD1" stores out of
! bounds with the system disabled.  BDSZ at 022517.00 branches if DS
! is already on (and zeroes it) -- its target 022517.40 is also its
! own fall-through, so that row is a pure CLEAR, a branch written for
! its side effect and going nowhere.  BZDSZ at 022520.00 then demands
! that the offending SX did NOT set it: disabled, the indicator does
! not actuate at all.  The second half enables the system and re-runs
! the same violation, this time checking the interrupt (the handler
! planted at 160107 vectors to 160126) AND the store suppression.
!
! THE POLARITY ABOVE IS A CORRECTION TO THIS FILE, and the reason it
! took a second pass is worth recording.  B<IND> branches when the
! indicator is ON; BZ<IND> branches when it is OFF.  That is one bit of
! the printed word -- enc_branch_ind puts branchOn at bit 30, inside
! the hex byte, 0xC6 against 0xC4 -- and classify_branch_indicator
! assigns it by prefix.  Read the other way round, every OK path in 8B
! and 8C becomes an error path and the whole block inverts.  The first
! draft of this file said "disabled, the indicator still sets, there is
! just no interruption", which is the opposite of what the deck asks.
!
! THE ERROR TEXT WAS THE TELL, and it is the same tell 8C_disabled
! already argues from: "-SX OUT OF BOUNDS SETS DS WHEN / -SYSTEM
! DISABLED." is a COMPLAINT.  It names the anomaly, not the
! requirement.  A message can only be read as a specification if you
! already know which path reaches it, and here the branch is the OK
! path and the fall-through is the SIC,SEN / B,SERS pair.
!
! BUT THE ARGUMENT DOES NOT HAVE TO REST ON READING A MESSAGE, and it
! should not, because reading a message is the same KIND of inference
! that produced the error.  A PRINTED ENCODING says it outright: this
! half is the disabled case because 022515.40 is "BD,$+.32" printing
! "22516.04 00" -- BRANCH DISABLED, two rows before the out-of-bounds
! store at 022517.40.  The second half re-enables with "BE,$+.32" at
! 022534.00 printing "22534.40 00".  Those two rows are in this file's
! bit-18 table already; they were sitting there while the prose above
! them said the opposite.  Prefer an encoding to a reading whenever
! one is available.
!
! HOW FAR THE CLAIM REACHES, stated exactly.  "Disabled means the
! protection indicator does not actuate at all" is witnessed BY THE
! DECKS for all three address alarms: DS here, and DF and IF by
! ax5b_160_test8c_disabled.strap's "BZDFZ ... DF must be OFF" and
! "BZIFZ ... IF must be OFF".  The REFERENCE MANUAL quote the model
! cites for it -- printed p.45, a store attempted "while the
! interruption system was enabled" -- is DS-SPECIFIC.  So DS has both
! a manual source and a deck source; DF and IF have a deck source and
! inherit the gating by symmetry with it.  protViolates gates all
! three on st.irqEnabled(), which is right, but say which support each
! one actually has rather than letting one quote cover three.
!
! NOTHING CAUGHT IT BECAUSE PROSE IS NOT EXECUTABLE.  The rows are
! bit-exact against the printed code and always were; the verifier
! checks encodings, not glosses.  The model was right too --
! StretchExecute.bsv's protViolates ends `st.irqEnabled() &&
! installed(a) && signalled` -- so this was a claim in a comment
! contradicting both the sibling transcription and the code it
! describes, sitting in a committed file that passed every check.
!
! NOTE THE INDICATOR-REGISTER CLEARING SEQUENCE at 022526.40-022531.40:
! "Z,$IND" then "L(BU),$IND" then "BRZ,$+2.0".  The deck does not
! trust the zero; it loads word 11 back as a 64-bit unsigned binary
! field and branches on Result Zero, and if the register would not
! clear it terminates the test outright rather than reporting a DS
! failure it could not attribute.  Those two L(BU) rows are the only
! full words in the block, printed "13.00 80 000000.20 50" and
! "23063.00 80 000000.20 50".
!
! THE BIT TABLE GAINS TWO MORE CONFIRMATIONS: 024522 + n gives BIT11
! -> 024535 and BIT57 -> 024613, and the printed rows here are
! 24535.32 B0 and 24613.02 10 / 24613.04 10.  Every constants-listing
! address behind that was read at 600 dpi, not off the 200 dpi survey
! render that found them -- p.114 for BIT0 -> 024522.00, BIT10 ->
! 024534.00 (so BIT11 -> 024535.00) and 100O -> 024503.00, p.115 for
! BIT57 -> 024613.00, BIT62 -> 024620.00 and BIT63 -> 024621.00.  With
! both ends of the table read at full resolution the rule is bracketed
! rather than inferred.  (ax5b_16086_ds.strap's own header records why
! the resolution is worth stating: 200 dpi once printed 021400.00
! where 600 dpi printed 021400.40.)
!
! 100O IS A LETTER O, NOT A ZERO, and the glyphs were measured rather
! than eyeballed.  On PDF p.114 the common-constants block runs 100LC,
! 100Z, 100O, 100VO, 100CO; at 600 dpi a digit zero is 42-47 px wide
! in that font and a letter O is 49-54, and the last glyph of 100O
! measures 51 against 42/42 for the two zeros before it.  Its own
! definition line reads "-INDX WD. OF ONES."  The operand on p.75
! measures the same way (42, 43, 49) -- though at 49 px that one sits
! only 2 px outside the digit range, so it is p.114 and the comment
! that carry the reading, not p.75 alone.
!
! TWO GLYPH CAVEATS THAT CHANGE NO BIT.  The leading character of every
! 160xxx label is a bare vertical stroke, and this printer sets digit 1
! and letter I identically (compare "160123" with "INDX"); reading them
! as digits is this project's standing convention, not a measurement.
! And by the same calibration SEN0's fourth glyph measures 54 px on
! p.114, which is letter-O territory, so the symbol may really be
! "SENO" -- kept as LSEN0 here to match ax5b_160_test8c.strap, and
! harmless either way since it is a SYN taken from the row that names
! it.
!
! NO CNOP IN THIS BLOCK -- unlike TEST 8C next door, nothing here is
! alignment-dependent, and every row is a plain half-word or the two
! full-word VFL loads.
!
! ENCODING ONLY.  Running it needs SSIP's 160S1/160SX1 harness and the
! 64-word interrupt table at 160IT.
!
! Transcription standard: bit-exact against the printed machine code,
! verify_ax5b_160_test8b_listing.py.

        SLC, (8)22513.0
!
! ---- the shared setup: plant the BD at 160107 for tests 8B, 8C, 8D
L160123 LX,  $X1, L160XW1          ! -SETUP BD, 160107 INTERRUPT TABLE
        LX,  $X2, L160K17          ! -FOR TESTS 8B, 8C, 8D
        SX,  $X2, L160IT($X1)      ! index-modified store into the table
        CB+, $X1, $-.32            ! count up, loop back one half-word
        Z,   $MASK
!
! ---- FIRST HALF: system DISABLED -- the indicator sets, no interrupt
L160125 BD,  $+.32                 ! -TEST 8B CHKS DS WITH SYSTEMS
        LX,  $X1, LBIT57           ! -DISABLED, AND ENABLED.
        SX,  $X1, 3.0              ! boundary control on
        BDSZ, $+.32                ! clear a stale DS
        SX,  $X1, L160AD1          ! THE STORE OUT OF BOUNDS
        BZDSZ, $+1.32              ! DS must be ON
        SIC, SEN                   ! -SX OUT OF BOUNDS SETS DS WHEN
        B,   SERS                  ! -SYSTEM DISABLED.
!
! ---- arm the interrupt path and clear the indicator register
        LVI, $X1, LINT
        LRI, $X1, L160IT
        LX,  $X2, LBIT57
        SX,  $X2, 3.0
        LVI, $X2, L160126
        SVA, $X2, L160107          ! plant the handler
        LVI, $X2, L160K11
        SV,  $X2, $UB              ! upper boundary := 160K11
        SIC, L160SX1
        BD,  L160S1                ! SSIP save/restore
        Z,   $IND
        L(BU), $IND                ! read word 11 back ...
        BRZ, $+2.0                 !   ... and demand it is zero
        SIC, SEN                   ! -UNABLE TO CLR IND REGS MUST
        B,   SERS                  ! -TERMINATE THIS TEST.
        B,   L160Z1
!
! ---- SECOND HALF: system ENABLED -- interrupt AND store suppression
        SR,  $X1, $IA
        Z,   $IND
        Z,   L160AD1               ! the target starts at zero
        LX,  $X2, L100O            ! the all-ones index word
        BE,  $+.32                 ! enable
        SX,  $X2, L160AD1          ! THE STORE ABOVE UB, ENABLED
        BD,  $+.32
        SV,  $X1, $IA
        SIC, SEN                   ! -NO INTERRUPT OCCURED ON AN
        B,   SERS                  ! -SX ABOVE UB.
L160126 L(BU), L160AD1             ! the interrupt lands here
        BRZ, $+1.32                ! the store must NOT have landed
        SIC, SEN                   ! -SX ABOVE UB WAS NOT NOPED, DATA
        B,   SERS                  ! -WAS STORED WHEN ENABLED.
        SV,  $X1, $IA
!
! ---- exit
L160Z1  B,   $+1.0
        BD,  L160123               ! loop back to the head
        SIC, LSEN0+.32
        B,   LSSW                  ! -TO SSIP.
        BD,  $+.32
!
! ---- the continuity bridge (test 8B done = bit 11)
        LX,  $X13, LIC260          ! -UPDATE CONTINUITY CHECK.
        V+,  $X13, LBIT11
        SX,  $X13, LIC260
!
! ---- externals, SYN'd at the addresses this listing's own encodings
!      give.  INPUTS to the check, not witnesses -- the same caveat
!      verify_ax5b_16082_16083_listing.py makes about L16088.  Each
!      was derived by reading bit 18 out of the octal pair of the row
!      that names it, so each is itself a checkable claim, but a row
!      whose operand is one of these verifies only the opcode and
!      modifier bits.  The rows that really check address placement
!      are the ones naming L160123, L160126 and L160Z1, which are
!      defined INSIDE the transcribed range.
L160XW1 SYN, (8)23065.0            ! from LX,$X1,160XW1  = 23065.02 10
L160K17 SYN, (8)23062.0            ! from LX,$X2,160K17  = 23062.04 10
L160K11 SYN, (8)23054.0            ! from LVI,$X2,160K11 = 23054.05 01
L160AD1 SYN, (8)23063.0            ! from Z,160AD1       = 23063.22 00
L160107 SYN, (8)22372.0            ! from SVA,$X2,160107 = 22372.05 D0
L160S1  SYN, (8)22741.0            ! SSIP save/restore
L160SX1 SYN, (8)23041.0
L160IT  SYN, (8)23201.0            ! the 64-word interrupt table
LINT    SYN, (8)1353.0
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0
LSEN0   SYN, (8)1311.0
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0
LSSW    SYN, (8)1301.0
LIC260  SYN, (8)22737.0            ! the continuity word
L100O   SYN, (8)24503.0            ! p.114's "INDX WD. OF ONES"
LBIT11  SYN, (8)24535.0            ! 024522 + 11
LBIT57  SYN, (8)24613.0            ! 024522 + 57 = the boundary control bit
        END, L160123
